An end-to-end review of quoting, delivery and invoicing for a family-owned wholesaler. See also [the benchmark that started it](/news/our-2026-sme-operations-benchmark/).

The wholesaler took about sixteen days to turn an order into a paid invoice. Quotes waited for approval by email, delivery notes were typed twice, and invoices were sent in batches at the end of the month. Nobody owned the process from the first call to the final payment.

We mapped the whole flow with the people who did each step. The map showed that the slowest part was not the warehouse, as everyone had assumed. It was the time between a quote and the signed order, where one manager had to approve every discount by hand.

We changed three things. Discounts below a set limit are now approved by the sales team on the spot. The dispatch team prints one packing slip, not two. And invoices go out on the day of delivery, instead of at the end of the month.

After six months, the lead time was about a third shorter, and the team could see every open order on one board. The owners now review the figures each month, and the process has an owner with a name.
